// PandaEye wire protocol v1: game-server plugin <-> cloud gateway.
//
// Transport: one WebSocket per game server (or proxy), path /v1/connect.
// Every WebSocket message is BINARY and starts with a one-byte codec header:
//   0x00 = the rest is a raw protobuf message
//   0x01 = the rest is a single zstd frame (with Frame_Content_Size set) wrapping the protobuf message
//   0x02 = the rest is a raw DEFLATE stream (RFC 1951, java.util.zip.Deflater(nowrap=true)) of the message;
//          plugins fall back to it when the pure-Java zstd codec is unavailable
// Plugin -> cloud messages are ClientFrame, cloud -> plugin messages are ServerFrame.
// Limits: compressed message <= 1 MiB, decompressed <= 8 MiB, Hello is always codec 0x00 and <= 16 KiB.
// The cloud never compresses ServerFrames in protocol v1 (they are small); plugins must still accept
// codecs 0x01 and 0x02 for forward compatibility.
//
// Session: the first ClientFrame must be Hello (within 10 s of the upgrade). The cloud answers with
// Welcome or with Error{fatal=true} and closes. After Welcome the plugin streams Batch frames.
// Every Batch is acknowledged with BatchAck once it is stored in the event stream (never before); the
// plugin keeps unacknowledged batches and resends them (replayed=true) after a reconnect. Delivery is
// at-least-once; the cloud de-duplicates by (tenant, server_id, seq). Rate limits are enforced as
// backpressure (the gateway stops reading and delays BatchAck), never by acknowledging dropped data.
//
// Time: every Event carries time_us = Unix time in microseconds derived from the plugin's monotonic
// clock (unix_us_at_start + (System.nanoTime() - nano_at_start) / 1000), so it never goes backwards
// and is comparable across events of one plugin process.
//
// UUIDs are 16 raw bytes, big-endian (most significant 64 bits first).

